							placeholder="you@publication.com"
							autocomplete="email"
							aria-describedby="sg-email-help sg-email-error"
							class="border-border bg-bg font-ui text-text w-full rounded-sm border px-3.5 py-3 text-[15px]">
						<p
							id="sg-email-help"
							class="font-ui text-muted mt-2 text-[13px]">Help text explains the field.</p>
						<p
							id="sg-email-error"
							class="font-ui text-danger mt-3.5 text-[13px]">Error state — enter a valid address.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</section>

			{{!-- ── 05 — Nav header ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
			The real menu, from `{{navigation}}`, inside a panel: the drawing shows the bar as a
			specimen, and a specimen built from hardcoded links would stop being one the moment a
			buyer edits their menu. The `current` marker is live too — whichever item is this page
			carries `aria-current` here exactly as it does in the bar above.

			The bar above is the same partial doing its real job; this panel is deliberately not
			`{{> "chrome/header"}}` again, because that partial owns `x-data="siteMenu"`, a skip
			link and the search trigger's id-free but singular contract with sodo-search. Two of
			them on one page would give the ⌘K shortcut two triggers and Alpine two drawers. --}}
			<section class="pt-18">
				<h2 class="font-ui text-muted mb-8 text-[13px] font-semibold tracking-[0.14em] uppercase">05 — Nav header</h2>

				<div class="border-border bg-surface overflow-hidden rounded-md border">
					<div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-[30px] gap-y-4 px-5 py-[22px] md:px-7">
						{{> "chrome/wordmark" wrapper="mr-auto" logoHeight="h-5" titleSize="text-2xl"}}

						<nav
							aria-label="{{t "Specimen menu"}}"
							class="max-nav:hidden flex flex-wrap items-center gap-[22px]">{{navigation variant="bar"}}</nav>

						<span class="btn btn-primary font-ui max-nav:hidden rounded-sm px-[18px] py-2.5 text-sm font-semibold">Subscribe</span>
					</div>

					<div class="bg-surface-2 border-border flex items-center gap-[18px] border-t px-5 py-3 md:px-7">
						<span class="text-muted font-mono text-[11px] tracking-[0.08em] uppercase">
							Mobile: hamburger → Alpine <code class="font-mono">x-data</code> drawer,
							<code class="font-mono">aria-expanded</code> bound, focus trapped, Esc closes.
						</span>
					</div>
				</div>
			</section>

			{{!-- ── 06 — Post card, author card, newsletter, pagination ──────────────────────
			Four specimens, three of them the theme's own partials driven by real records — a card
			that renders invented data is not a specimen of anything a buyer will see.

			The pagination control is inside its own `{{#get}}` on purpose: `{{pagination}}`
			reports the pagination of the query it sits in, so a page-2 query of the real archive
			gives the control real numbers to draw. Outside a get it would read this Page's own
			context, which has one page and therefore renders nothing.

			TWO DEVIATIONS FROM THE DRAWING, both because a partial owns its own shape. The
			drawing sets all four in one 3-up row; the newsletter band is `container`-scoped and
			nested containers do not compose, so it takes its own full-width row below. And the
			author card is written out here rather than lifted from `directory/author-cards`,
			which is a whole `<section>` with its own heading and query — the portrait, however,
			is that Surface's partial, so a face renders the same way it does everywhere. --}}
			<section class="pt-18">
				<h2 class="font-ui text-muted mb-8 text-[13px] font-semibold tracking-[0.14em] uppercase">
					06 — Post card, author card, newsletter, pagination
				</h2>

				<div class="grid grid-cols-[repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(320px,100%),1fr))] gap-6">
					{{#get "posts" limit="1" include="authors,tags" order="published_at desc" as |specimen|}}
						{{#if specimen}}
							{{#foreach specimen}}
								{{> "archive/card" tagEyebrow=true}}
							{{/foreach}}
						{{/if}}
					{{/get}}

					<div class="flex flex-col gap-6">
						{{#get "authors" limit="1" include="count.posts" as |writer|}}
							{{#if writer}}
								{{#foreach writer}}
									<article class="border-border bg-surface rounded-md border p-[26px] text-center">
										{{> "authors/portrait" size="h-[72px] w-[72px]" px="72" textSize="text-[22px]" extra="mx-auto"}}

										<h3 class="font-display mt-4 text-[22px] font-medium">{{name}}</h3>

										<p class="font-meta text-meta tracking-meta text-muted mt-2 uppercase">
											{{~plural count.posts empty=(t "No posts") singular=(t "% post") plural=(t "% posts")~}}
										</p>

										{{#if bio}}
											<p class="text-small text-muted mt-3.5 leading-[1.6]">{{bio}}</p>
										{{/if}}

										<div class="mt-[18px] flex justify-center gap-2.5">
											<a
												href="{{url}}"
												class="btn border-border font-ui text-text hover:bg-surface-2 rounded-sm border px-4 py-[9px] text-[13px] font-semibold no-underline">{{t "All posts"}}</a>
											<a
												href="#newsletter-specimen"
												class="btn btn-primary font-ui rounded-sm px-4 py-[9px] text-[13px] font-semibold no-underline">{{t "Follow"}}</a>
										</div>
									</article>
								{{/foreach}}
							{{/if}}
						{{/get}}

						{{#get "posts" limit="4" page="2"}}
							{{pagination bare=true pt="pt-0"}}
						{{/get}}
					</div>
				</div>

				{{> "newsletter" anchor="newsletter-specimen" pt="pt-6"}}
			</section>

			{{!-- ── 07 — Rendered post content ───────────────────────────────────────────────
			The editor's own output, styled by the `.mirror-content` layer. Everything above this
			line is the theme documenting itself; everything below it is a Page body a buyer can
			edit, which is why the card set is a fixture rather than markup in this file. --}}
			<section class="pt-18">
				<h2 class="font-ui text-muted mb-3 text-[13px] font-semibold tracking-[0.14em] uppercase">
					07 — Rendered post content
				</h2>
				<p class="text-muted mb-10 font-mono text-xs">
					Measure locked to 68ch. Ghost card types below map 1:1 to the editor's card set.
				</p>

				<div class="mirror-content measure mx-auto">
					{{content}}
				</div>
			</section>
		</article>
	{{/post}}
</main>

{{!-- The four font names, read from the cascade rather than written down.

Ghost lets a buyer pick a heading and a body font in admin, which arrive as `--gh-font-heading` and
`--gh-font-body` and are resolved through the fallbacks in assets/css/screen.css. A hardcoded
"Newsreader" would be wrong the moment someone changes it, and there is no Handlebars expression for
"the family this element resolved to" — so the one thing this sheet cannot state, it asks the
browser for. With scripting off the four cells are empty and the specimens still show the faces,
which is the honest failure. --}}
<script>
	for (const cell of document.querySelectorAll('[data-family]')) {
		cell.textContent = getComputedStyle(cell).fontFamily.split(',')[0].replace(/["']/g, '');
	}
</script>

{{> "chrome/footer"}}
